what's up guys this is international  master Eric Rosen and welcome to another  video in this video I'd like to respond  to a YouTube commenter whose name is  Oliver Kay boster she is a pretty boss  name and he was asking about what to do  in the Sicilian when they don't play an  early d4 but they play an early night of  3 in Bishop c4 so the line he gave me  was efore c5 Knight of 3 d6  and Bishop c4 and he told me he's a  dragon player and he encounters this I  guess on an occasional basis and he's  not quite sure what to do so I will say  that Bishop c4 is completely fine move  and you're not gonna be able to be  winning after this move but I would  recommend maybe playing something a  little bit different than what you're  used to in kind of a normal dragon now  normally if you are a dragon player you  can go ahead and play something like g6  Bishop e7 and io6 and castle and got a  completely playable position but what I  would recommend against Bishop c4 is e 6  and the idea is to exploit Bishop c4 as  much as possible and what you're doing  with e6 is you are undermining the  bishop so it's not as effective just  kind of staring at your solid pawn chain  and a long-term idea for black in this  opening is to eventually play this move  d5 so let's say white will continue  let's say like castles and now the moves  I'm playing here for white are just kind  of based on what I've seen  beginners play I have a lot of students  who might play against this as black and  I see a lot of white players just kind  of playing kind of standard normal  developing loose and what I want to show  is a potential trap that white could  walk into so let's play some some normal  moves for for both sides let's say d3 I  usually like to  the bishop on e7 I should note that if  you're gonna put your pawn on e6 usually  you shouldn't combine the movie six with  the move g6 because it's a usually a  little bit too weakening so this should  be seven very standard here Knight c3  Castle and let's say white walks into  the trap now one of the most natural  looking moves here for white is actually  a terrible terrible mistake that move is  Bishop to e3 and from this point on  black is uh is winning material and if  you haven't seen this before it can be  so much shocking and I would encourage  those who haven't seen this before to  pause your video and find the material  winning move for black so the move is  very simply pawn d5 and the point is  that black is going to end up forking  white so if white takes and black takes  d5 or d4 is coming and white has to lose  a minor piece no matter what so I fish  up to be three d5 let's say Bishop take  take black has won a piece for a pond  with a significant edge and it won't be  all the time that whites will fall into  this trap but it's always nice to have  kind of a a trap that your opponent  might fall into and it's always nice to  know an opening where there is a trap  because sometimes it's a bit more  motivational to play it let's say white  doesn't fall into the trap and let's say  white plays perhaps a more standard or  better remove like rook e1 blacks plan  is simply to continue development black  wants to play knight c6 and the  long-term plan for black in this  position is something you'll see a lot  especially and maybe more close to  sealing positions is black wants to  expand on the Queen side and the very  simple idea is to play six b5 and  sometimes that can be supported as well  with rook the eights and if we perhaps  play some moves for whites let's say  white doesn't play Bishop e3 which would  be bad again but plays some move like  this  to g5 then very simply a6 afy prevents  this let's say a for this will  occasionally happen  what black can do another fan is to play  Knight to d4 and this is a move which if  you play the Sicilian you should kind of  become accustom with it's a move to not  only try and gain more space but be a  bit more aggressive and and create some  some pressure in White's position and  you should not be scared of night take  t4 because after a pawn take d4 yes you  do have double pawns but after this move  92 you have a few different ways of  playing here maybe the the most obvious  way is to play e5 and just kind of  secure your pawns and really there's not  too many problems with this position for  black buy the more aggressive way of  playing and the move I really like here  is pawn d5 and what this does is it  kicks the bishop  if kwon-taek g5 you can actually take  with Knights and some trades might  happen but but then your pawn on d4 is  well reinforced and it's not a bad  position for black so I think I hope  some of this answered answered what to  do against Bishop c4 I think kind of  knowing your piece placement is one  thing that will be helpful in the  opening but then the other thing is kind  of understanding how your setup will  dictate your plans and in this case  especially with sealing positions these  plans of expanding on Queen side and  going for central play are important to  know and it's the type of position where  if you kind of know it ahead of time  then you can play it over the board and  I have to be trying to come up with  ideas over the board so I hope that  helped for people who might have kind of  similar questions feel free comment  below and I will see you guys in the  next video

